          "passage": "Monthly Temperatures and Wing Centroid Sizes of Fruit Fly Specimens\nMonth Average\nhigh (°F)\nAverage\nlow (°F)\nAverage male wing\ncentroid size (mm)\nAverage female wing\ncentroid size (mm)\nOctober 67 44 1.98 2.29\nMay 73 50 1.98 2.27\nJuly 87 62 2.02 2.31\nSeptember 80 54 1.98 2.27\nDrosophila (fruit flies) have generation times of\n10–12 days, so seasonal changes in humidity and\nother environmental conditions can drive\nseasonal fluctuations in chromosome\nrearrangements in species such asD. persimilis\nand D. subobscura. Drosophila body size (for\nwhich wing centroid size serves as a proxy\nmeasure) correlates with reproductive fitness.\nBanu Şebnem Önder and Cansu Fidan Aksoy\nmeasured the wing sizes of members of aD.\nmelanogaster population in Yeşilöz, Turkey, that\nwere collected monthly between May and\nOctober over three years. Their research\nsuggests thatDrosophila collected in relatively\nwarmer months should tend to have greater\nreproductive fitness, as is illustrated by the\nfinding that _____",
