          "passage": "Composition and Fracture Toughness Five HEAs\nHEA\nidentification\nnumber\nComposition\n(%)\nFracture toughness\n(megapascals times\nthe square root of crack length)\n15\nchromium (33.33),\ncobalt (33.33),\nnickel (33.33)\n265.20\n7\nchromium (20),\ncobalt (20),\niron (20),\nmanganese (20),\nnickel (20)\n219.00\n25\naluminum (1.07),\ncarbon (46.78),\nchromium (1.07),\ncobalt (1.07),\ncopper (1.07),\niron (1.07),\nnickel (1.07),\ntungsten (46.78)\n10.41\n95\naluminum (20),\ncobalt (20),\ncopper (20),\nnickel (20),\nzinc (20)\n4.45\n51\nmolybdenum (25),\nniobium (25),\ntantalum (25),\ntungsten (25)\n3.30\nHigh-entropy alloys (HEAs) have been observed\nto have greater fracture toughness – greater\nresistance to crack propagation – than\nconventional alloys. It has been proposed that\nfracture toughness increases with the proportion\nof an HEA consisting of cobalt, but data on\nHEAs compiled by engineer Xuesong Fan show\nthat this is not the case; for example,\n_______",
          "prompt": "Which choice most effectively uses data from the\ntable to complete the text?",
          "options": [
